From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander <4418484+kuscher@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 07:37:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/19] Android: Fusion under Patchy's style, like the Linux
 build

Qt's default base style on Android is its "android" style, which Patchy's
stylesheets don't target (spin boxes drew no text). Fusion is what the
Linux build gets.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/app/main.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/app/main.cpp b/src/app/main.cpp
index 7681374..1417a6d 100644
--- a/src/app/main.cpp
+++ b/src/app/main.cpp
@@ -401,7 +401,14 @@ int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
   QGuiApplication::setDesktopFileName(QStringLiteral("com.rtsoft.patchy"));
 #endif
   // Slider grooves snap to the click; tool flyouts open on a short hold (see the style above).
+#ifdef Q_OS_ANDROID
+  // On Android the default base style is Qt's "android" style, which Patchy's
+  // stylesheets don't target (its spin boxes draw no text); Fusion is what the
+  // Linux build gets.
+  app.setStyle(new InteractionHintsStyle(QStringLiteral("Fusion")));
+#else
   app.setStyle(new InteractionHintsStyle);
+#endif
   app.setApplicationName(QStringLiteral("Patchy"));
   app.setApplicationVersion(QStringLiteral(PATCHY_VERSION));
   // Keep the internal app identity for settings without letting Qt append " - Patchy" to every native window title.
